#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167962} :activity/id #uuid "f98be8d2-8501-53a4-b5df-d4f1ac4126c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050260 :topic/id #uuid "3a43c523-581b-58c8-a48d-f665d05c6e1f" :topic/math-academy-id 3218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168050 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A.$ \n![](images/c1/c12d2f59eb7eb9730fc7576bb426cff90a3cff4ac8577c3f212db8101197300b.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n![](images/4d/4dbe29e205fecf97bd7d4c0bfbf810c7336a6a50198fa35319e7ae8b0ac505c0.svg)\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "83dcb8a1-56ef-59d0-addc-dfaa18628695" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14816 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "691d9e29-1625-5c4d-8958-88262d0bc24b" :step/math-academy-id 610829 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166577 :question/id #uuid "5ebf00aa-406d-5286-b4ae-0f0044ce9b4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144f097c-0080-5857-b992-8996f194ca22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521540}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions to Check Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "9565a3bd-15ac-5aa0-88fe-2e6938ac1515" :step/math-academy-id 610830 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168059 :tutorial/content "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A].\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A.$ \n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n![](images/cd/cd01819adb510c2b439f44089b1bd3cbd1f49e671779039fdf2f6be62a39810f.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a719d20-4e48-50a9-9e39-db7296bd47d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14942 :tutorial/title "Set Notation and Bracket Notation"} :step/id #uuid "c85ef936-9305-53f6-8db5-6415695e3c58" :step/math-academy-id 610833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166579 :question/id #uuid "c831e4d2-736d-5ee1-9c0e-55cee535f361" :question/math-academy-id "e-22020"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e434dbd-b33c-5997-b3ba-043233cfbc89" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554632}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Indicator Notations"} :step/id #uuid "3c0f9089-52c4-5d6c-bd45-8b5935705854" :step/math-academy-id 610834 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168051 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0,$ the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i.$ If $x_i \\leq 0,$ the indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values $x_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2.$ Using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1[5>0]\\cdot 5+1[-3>0]\\cdot (-3)+1[2>0]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+0+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]$ counts the positive values. For this data, the count is $1 + 0 + 1 = 2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc59a9ec-2473-5942-9ab7-f81210d7426c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14817 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Sums"} :step/id #uuid "68e458ec-f61c-5b8a-ad94-e1c6511e3aeb" :step/math-academy-id 610837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166578 :question/id #uuid "1687d5ff-6b48-52d9-a947-3425227af366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1900018f-1bac-5623-b45f-e3d9c89f5d74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554636}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "559e173c-cc9a-5be6-9a22-191e4f99a000" :step/math-academy-id 610838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168060 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n![](images/33/336dc8080661db77e7ac701204aa6a32075bc7f4bd06625f4e8467e87a5c075c.svg)\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n|x| = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nSince the conditions $x \\geq 0$ and $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0.$ This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-4| & =1[-4\\ge 0]\\cdot (-4)+1[-4<0]\\cdot (-(-4)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ebf85-232e-5ea7-aed0-188bbb2467bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14963 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ec8855d0-be42-5c66-a902-f6d870071621" :step/math-academy-id 611696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167967}}] :activity/title "Indicator Functions for Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167001}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554661} :activity/id #uuid "465539fa-aa06-5c88-a039-a2b61aa93569" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050260 :topic/id #uuid "3a43c523-581b-58c8-a48d-f665d05c6e1f" :topic/math-academy-id 3218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd697bf3-bab3-5be7-93b9-af592dc078ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554352} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb32b44-fa3a-53b5-9c67-b4cbe35e057e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-347438" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=7\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1$ equals $2{x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $1$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1\n$$\n\nadds $2{x}_{i}$ for nonnegative values and $1$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-6\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[-1\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[4\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[7\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1 & =1+1+2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =1+1+0+8+14 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554663}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156a71be-c9a4-5ae7-9a2c-02e2f98c3a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553911} :answer-field/id #uuid "034b7137-7dce-507f-82b0-3f725c6f8c4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346677" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=9,\\,{x}_{2}=-8,\\,{x}_{3}=-1,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-8>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[4>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9+0+0+4+2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2555c50a-befa-5681-b2d0-0c5336c8f67e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a42e783-06ac-540f-a993-5fee32a5d1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[-1=C]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554115} {:answer/id #uuid "905baf65-bc02-52ab-a96c-06c275629272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[-1\\notin \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554181} {:answer/id #uuid "c64a4829-6ad8-5110-9db6-5fd9a585d15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[C\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554304} {:answer/id #uuid "c77659db-76b7-535a-9c58-254c1f6de06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554307} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d06cfb-9a89-5639-8808-7411f0dd598c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}\\in -1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554307} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c55f3e7-608f-546e-9238-bec098222d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346662" :question/problem "Let $C=\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{C}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{C}(x)$ of a set $C$ equals $1$ if $x\\in C$ and $0$ if $x\\notin C.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(x)=1[x\\in C].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(-1)=1[-1\\in C]=1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3398d19e-2a4e-5ffe-8497-c4c076f1a2b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9e8e21-b550-5dab-acbe-bf94504b1d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{\\{2,4,6,8\\}}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553901} {:answer/id #uuid "23fcb709-e1df-5686-a023-45de9c7332ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{5}(B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553932} {:answer/id #uuid "6acf5d37-f233-58a7-b644-4950c5f08b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{5}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554078} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec50b2b-0fb1-535c-bee6-07f2da381e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{B}(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554088} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7b8b0c-b17c-5fc2-aad1-cf9ecfd4c52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{1}_{B}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554420} :answer-field/id #uuid "68354444-5465-5b9a-9ef0-8c6ea884ef5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6df0b1a8-9045-561a-b52c-2dfb375e5b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-349167" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[5\\in B]?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a5fb79e-7cd3-5538-834f-01836d139a39"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d52dde-78fc-56d2-a22f-16743942f394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[\\{0,5,10\\}\\in 7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553963} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5a4ce9-38a3-50c2-ab37-2b31fa255565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[7=D]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553982} {:answer/id #uuid "3f98e784-15fe-5da6-bf43-5d7bde990e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[D\\in \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553984} {:answer/id #uuid "929abffe-450c-5006-a4e0-9188f9b6910d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554193} {:answer/id #uuid "caeec9e3-866d-5050-ab7c-4b6736d7497b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1[7\\notin \\{0,5,10\\}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554193} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8b1eb9-905f-5eaa-b94d-45d4a5b08f18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346663" :question/problem "Let $D=\\{0,5,10\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{D}(7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{D}(x)$ of a set $D$ equals $1$ if $x\\in D$ and $0$ if $x\\notin D.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(x)=1[x\\in D].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(7)=1[7\\in D]=1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bfd99f70-2dec-5500-aa3b-df54e1e74a81" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554656}}] :activity/title "Indicator Functions for Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553844}]}
